2. Source audit: investigate these exact integration points

Use the actual current files and verify the findings below before changing them.

Terrain and movement

* src/scene/terrain.ts, especially getWalkableSurface() around lines 287–361, currently prioritizes the bridge, stepping stones, water and ordinary terrain using horizontal position. It does not select a surface based on the character’s height or vertical direction.
* src/scene/characterController.ts uses getWalkableSurface(x, z) and its airborne landing logic lands whenever the character’s Y position is at or below the returned ground height. A character below an elevated surface can therefore snap upward if that surface is selected incorrectly.
* The controller’s current nominal jump impulse is 7.2, gravity is 21.0, and the existing movement code includes grounded/airborne velocity interpolation. Verify all values and logic against the actual branch.
* The controller currently derives terrain pitch from -surface.normal.z * 0.38, clamped to approximately ±0.28 radians, and derives dynamic banking from heading change. This is not a sufficient general solution for diagonal ramps and banked surfaces.
* resolveCollision() in terrain.ts uses X/Z obstacle circles and a radius-48 boundary. Its obstacle collisions are not height-aware, and its near-zero-distance case should be handled safely.
* The controller queries the surface more than once per update. Avoid adding expensive allocations or global scans to this hot path.

Camera

* src/scene/thirdPersonCamera.ts currently uses velocity-aware facing, smoothing and a bounded look-ahead of roughly 1.8 metres. Its obstruction checks are terrain-oriented and do not adequately account for elevated course geometry.
* Extend this camera; do not replace it with an abrupt rail camera or force cinematic rotations that override manual orbit.

Character model loading

* src/scene/meadowScene.ts loads the optional GLTF knight asynchronously and replaces the active controller when it finishes.
* The replacement copies position and yaw but does not preserve the complete movement state. It can reset velocity, airborne state, jump buffering and other logical state if loading completes during gameplay.
* The asynchronous callback has no sufficient destroyed-scene guard. Invalid assets or failures during preparation must not leak resources or leave partially inserted scene objects.
* Prefer a stable physics root with a replaceable visual child. The controller should continue to own position, velocity, grounded state, jump state and course progress while the visual model and animation mixer are replaced. If that is too invasive, implement complete explicit state transfer; copying only position and yaw is not sufficient.

Resource ownership

Verify and repair the actual disposal paths in:

* src/scene/heroCharacter.ts: procedural character geometry and materials must be released.
* src/scene/bridgeSystem.ts: bridge-owned geometry as well as materials must be released.
* src/scene/environment.ts: removing landmarkGroup does not dispose its descendants’ geometries/materials. Trees share materials, while other landmarks own their materials. Dispose owned resources once, with explicit ownership.
* src/scene/dustSystem.ts: it creates shared base sprite materials and cloned particle materials; dispose the base materials too.
* src/scene/meadowScene.ts: retain and dispose the PMREM render target, dispose the RoomEnvironment after baking, and clean up loaded GLTF resources.
* All affected dispose() methods must be safe to call repeatedly. Do not blindly dispose shared resources once per mesh.

The TreeInstance interface in treeSystem.ts must not be treated as proof that returned trees have a dispose() method: verify the actual return type and implement cleanup accordingly.

