3. Architecture: one source of truth

Create a clear, maintainable course system. You may use src/scene/platformerCourse.ts plus a separate course runtime module, or another architecture if it is demonstrably better.

Separate these responsibilities:

1. Course data/generator: deterministic graph, nodes, segments, widths, elevation profiles, route choices, checkpoints, launch/landing metadata and finish state.
2. Course geometry: rendered top surfaces, underside/thickness where required, rails, supports, path markings and junction meshes.
3. Surface resolver: gameplay support candidates derived from the same sampled geometry.
4. Route progression: current route/segment, branch choice, checkpoint and finish handling.
5. Camera context: upcoming turns, ramps, landing targets, banked sections and reveals.
6. Resource ownership: explicit, idempotent cleanup for every owned object.

Use per-scene instances and dependency injection. Do not add a mutable global course singleton or create a circular dependency in which terrain.ts imports a course module that itself imports terrain.

Generate the course data once per scene using a deterministic seed. Use an authored, constrained route topology with controlled procedural variation—not random independent platform placement. A fixed default seed must always produce the same course. A developer-only seed override may be added for reproducible testing.

The rendered geometry, course collision, route metadata, ground-path markings and camera context must all derive from the same course data.

