5. Geometry and procedural constraints

Use cubic curves with controlled handles and stable tangent behavior. Use parallel-transport frames or an equally robust method to build the ribbon cross-sections without twisting.

Requirements:

* Adaptive sampling, denser around curvature changes, ramp crests, junctions and landing edges.
* Smooth elevation and normal transitions. No uncontrolled spline overshoot or zero-length segments.
* Shared junction geometry at the split and merge; no overlapping coplanar strips, visible cracks or mismatched heights.
* Merged geometry by material where appropriate. Do not create a separate mesh for every sample or tiny segment.
* Instanced repeated supports and markers where practical.
* Stable segment/node IDs and explicit graph connectivity. Every intended route must reach the finish.
* Seeded variation must preserve the course topology and constraints. A failed generation attempt must retry a bounded number of times or fall back to the validated default; it must never silently emit an invalid course.

Sample the actual generated spline, not just control points, and validate the entire route width, rails, landing footprint, supports, height profile and boundary clearance.

Suggested starting constraints, subject to verification against the existing controller:

* Default walkable route width around 3 metres, wider at junctions and landing platforms.
* Maximum sustained route grade around 16 degrees unless the controller is deliberately and safely extended.
* Bank angle initially around 10–12 degrees.
* Minimum sprint-turn radius around 7 metres except at designed junction transitions.
* At least 0.5 metres of obstacle safety margin after accounting for route half-width and character radius.
* No unintended water intersections, boundary violations or route self-intersections. The only allowed multi-level overlaps must be explicitly designed and tested.

6. Surface resolution and character physics

Do not simply make the course mesh take priority over terrain.

Implement a state-aware candidate-surface resolver. It must consider:

* Base terrain, existing bridge, stepping stones, water and course surfaces.
* Character Y position, grounded state and vertical velocity.
* Previous support surface/segment ID.
* Whether the character is descending and has actually crossed a candidate surface from above.
* Height and normal continuity at ramps, landings and junctions.

Keep the existing base terrain query usable for callers that only need a height estimate. Inject the state-aware resolver into the controller rather than creating a circular import or global registry.

Required behavior:

* A character standing on the upper course stays on the upper surface.
* A character underneath it stays on the lower surface and is never teleported upward.
* A jump below a deck does not trigger a landing on the deck.
* A character falling onto a platform from above lands only when its trajectory crosses that platform while descending.
* Missing a jump can lead to the lower route or terrain and recovery, rather than an invisible death/reset.
* Existing bridge and stepping-stone traversal remains correct.
* Existing water hazards remain correct when the character is actually supported by the water/stream bed.
* At a shared merge, both routes meet at the same world height and the surface resolver does not flicker between IDs.

Use the same sampled geometry to build the rendered top surface and gameplay surface query. Compare query heights/normals against the actual generated mesh. Do not store references to mutable scratch normals; copy values when they must persist.

Jump verification

Extract a pure physics helper only if it helps the runtime and tests share the same actual rules. Do not write a second approximate jump formula and use that as proof.

Simulate the actual controller’s jump and horizontal movement with the course’s takeoff state, input, gravity, air control, landing height and platform footprint at 30, 60 and 120 FPS, plus the controller’s maximum delta-time case. Include both full run-up and a slower approach.

The candidate landing pad should allow the actual trajectory to intersect the platform with meaningful margin from the near edge. If the jump is not reachable, first adjust the gap, ramp, landing platform or run-up. Change the existing jump parameters only if the measured tests demonstrate that a change is necessary; preserve the existing movement feel.

Surface alignment

Replace the current Z-normal-only pitch approximation with a calculation based on the surface normal relative to the character’s forward direction. Combine the surface’s cross-slope bank with the existing dynamic turn-bank effect without double-counting it.

Test ordinary terrain and ramps aligned with X, aligned with Z, diagonal ramps, the banked turn, takeoff, landing and underpass. Keep the current meadow movement visually unchanged within a reasonable tolerance.

